<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elizabeth Charlotte, Madame Palatine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1652">1652</a>–<a href="/years/1722">1722</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Liselotte von der Pfalz, Elizabeth Charlotte of the Palatinate · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elizabeth Charlotte of the Palatinate, known as Liselotte, was a daughter of Elector Charles I Louis who married Philippe, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, in 1671. When her brother Charles II died without heirs in 1685, Louis XIV claimed Palatine lands in her name, a pretext for the Nine Years' War and the devastation of the Palatinate. She is also remembered for thousands of frank letters describing life at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-charlotte-madame-palatine.jpg" alt="Elizabeth Charlotte, Madame Palatine" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Pierre Mignard I · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liselotte,_Duchess_of_Orl%C3%A9ans_in_1675_by_Mignard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elizabeth Charlotte was born on 27 May 1652 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> castle, the second child and only daughter of Charles I Louis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, and Charlotte of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>. Nicknamed Liselotte, she was raised in the Reformed Protestant faith. Her parents' marriage went badly, and in 1657 her father separated from her mother to marry Marie Luise von Degenfeld morganatically. She remained close to several of her thirteen half-siblings, the Raugrafen, and kept up a lifelong correspondence with two half-sisters, Louise and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalie-elisabeth-of-hanau-munzenberg">Amalie Elisabeth</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1671 she was married to Philippe I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, known as Monsieur, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and twelve years her senior. Her aunt Anna Gonzaga negotiated the contract, including the terms of her conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. The wedding by proxy took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> on 16 November 1671, the day after she renounced her Reformed faith, and she first met her husband on 20 November at Châlons. As his wife she was styled Madame. At first she became very close to the king, who enjoyed her wit, her dancing and her habit of long walks and hunting.</p><p>From about 1680 her husband's favourites, the Chevalier de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and the Marquis d'Effiat among them, intrigued against her and had her confidantes dismissed. Her relationship with the king cooled as Madame de Maintenon gained influence, and she withdrew into her writing room. The Duke died of a stroke at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cloud">Saint-Cloud</a> on 9 June 1701 after an argument with the king. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> died on 1 September 1715, her son Philippe II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> overturn the king's will and became Regent, and she became first lady of the court. She had three children and died on 8 December 1722.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Liselotte is said to have written about 60,000 letters, two thirds in German and one third in French; about 5,000 survive, some 850 of them in French. They give vivid, often satirical accounts of court life, memories of her German childhood, gossip from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and thoughts on literature and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Writing to relatives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> also eased her melancholy and kept up her German, a link to her home and identity.</p><p>Selections first appeared in French in 1788 and in German soon after, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> she was read as a witness to the depravity of the old regime. An anonymous edition of 1791 presented her as the good, honest German woman. Through her children she was ancestress of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and of many European royal families, and has been called the Grandmother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elizabeth Charlotte, Madame Palatine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palatinate suffered again in the Nine Years' War, when Louis XIV's troops, pressing the inheritance claims of <b>Elizabeth Charlotte</b>, duchess of Orléans, systematically burned Heidelberg, Mannheim, Speyer and Worms in 1689, and Heidelberg Castle was blown up in 1689 and 1693.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-castle">Heidelberg Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Château de Saint-Cloud, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.41,8.72&amp;year=1697" aria-label="Heidelberg Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Elizabeth Charlotte, Madame Palatine"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5483 5869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5483 5869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.41,8.72&amp;year=1697">What was here in 1697? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
